d}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =36\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97672/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97672"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-97568"] :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{2}\\,i-\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-6\\,i-6\\sqrt{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97671/selection/0dad13d42d6fb05255ca2ae3276edcc611ec971d826a3928fbc197a9507b2a6f" :answer/id #uuid "98b890d3-54f5-5388-b4b6-069620f16038" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97671/selection/22fb7fee5d33448e28ff11b6b9987949f2b87d95ecbe0fec382add4ca2baa591" :answer/id #uuid "ffa63a01-f747-5722-8358-079f9f614966" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97671/selection/003c40e53d643d7a4d358fc68b93b85ab18da8cee29414f156eba7ae800987ad" :answer/id #uuid "c709a099-dfbf-51c1-83a0-1774749f6dcc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97671/selection/53c7301fd8e87fc621d5577e4102cd25eacac49af9631ec140128f0469e05aa8" :answer/id #uuid "1a5b55af-2e8a-55ae-8df8-63ce6cfc4caf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}i+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97671/selection/c5431ebca341db5f476511121ced21fd84f3de1b101ffcb85595ef7694cb7e66" :answer/id #uuid "2c69d569-5bb8-554b-80a3-cf88826a01ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97671/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e477cef2-d4df-5e9d-aa37-503abefe3a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97671/selection/003c40e53d643d7a4d358fc68b93b85ab18da8cee29414f156eba7ae800987ad" :answer-field/choices ["q-97671/selection/0dad13d42d6fb05255ca2ae3276edcc611ec971d826a3928fbc197a9507b2a6f" "q-97671/selection/22fb7fee5d33448e28ff11b6b9987949f2b87d95ecbe0fec382add4ca2baa591" "q-97671/selection/003c40e53d643d7a4d358fc68b93b85ab18da8cee29414f156eba7ae800987ad" "q-97671/selection/53c7301fd8e87fc621d5577e4102cd25eacac49af9631ec140128f0469e05aa8" "q-97671/selection/c5431ebca341db5f476511121ced21fd84f3de1b101ffcb85595ef7694cb7e66"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97671" :question/id #uuid "35cbae5d-df52-57c7-8623-19ca9f0bd498" :question/math-academy-id "q-97671" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(2,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=2$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{2}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,i-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97671/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97671"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97668/selection/f0cfd8c23bdb337e809b8439f1359ea6720e934fc72fb153825d52c068a24339" :answer/id #uuid "ea6ade0a-7b2b-54e1-807e-0edb1e4459fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97668/selection/33abccbb767addf718fd954c8c85066179e2aa0bd7a9add3c72f943b27feb156" :answer/id #uuid "44ab6e5f-6294-57f7-b518-1fbe44525f3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97668/selection/a74318b1d4a7b6329d6cdb9309d30f677e00eed255b5399d864a2666fb6cff58" :answer/id #uuid "abb6419f-a600-56fe-8e52-c7db9c99a2c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97668/selection/5ed7ede8510edaa75e00748d22522e5983bbbabd5e6d239538494752cd15de2c" :answer/id #uuid "2baf3fd0-8d84-5ae4-877b-569a5cfae13a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97668/selection/6765b4aeae8ba170d978922dc201c92cd8a2096c4f1449b7363f20c80434563d" :answer/id #uuid "8376623e-19fa-53d4-b951-88aa5cc7f90b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97668/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "839c81de-7b6e-5b66-b17f-3e29701e1192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97668/selection/a74318b1d4a7b6329d6cdb9309d30f677e00eed255b5399d864a2666fb6cff58" :answer-field/choices ["q-97668/selection/f0cfd8c23bdb337e809b8439f1359ea6720e934fc72fb153825d52c068a24339" "q-97668/selection/33abccbb767addf718fd954c8c85066179e2aa0bd7a9add3c72f943b27feb156" "q-97668/selection/a74318b1d4a7b6329d6cdb9309d30f677e00eed255b5399d864a2666fb6cff58" "q-97668/selection/5ed7ede8510edaa75e00748d22522e5983bbbabd5e6d239538494752cd15de2c" "q-97668/selection/6765b4aeae8ba170d978922dc201c92cd8a2096c4f1449b7363f20c80434563d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97668" :question/id #uuid "93547497-1bc4-5a24-a92c-ea680655d623" :question/math-academy-id "q-97668" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}+(-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡4t+{16}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=1\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={4}^{2}\\cdot 1=16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-97668/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97668"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8642"] :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0),$ we have that $\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and $\\omega= -3  \\textrm{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8643"] :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)^{2}+(5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+25{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t)} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = R|\\omega|,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = 5\\cdot |\\pi| = 5\\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8644"] :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\,  \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{36}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}} \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nNote that $||\\mathbf a||$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad ||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2||\\mathbf r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = 3^2\\cdot |4| = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
